    "title": "How to watch NASA’s Artemis II splashdown live Friday",
    "text": "NASA’s Artemis II crew has completed its primary mission objectives and is now preparing to return to Earth Friday, April 10. (Photo by NASA via Getty Images)Photo by NASA via Getty Images)NASA/NASA via Getty ImagesNASA’s Artemis II crew has completed its primary mission objectives and is set to return to Earth in a matter of hours. People can watch the crew's splashdown live. Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢The 10-day mission will conclude Friday with a planned splashdown off the California coast. NASA expects the crew to land in the Pacific Ocean near San Diego at 7:07 p.m. CT Friday.Here’s how to watch the Artemis II crew return to Earth.What time is Artemis II splashdown? Date: Friday, April 10, 2026 Broadcast begins: 5:30 p.m. CT Splashdown: approximately 7:07 p.m. CT Location: Off the coast of San Diego, California Article continues below this adNASA will provide a livestream of the Artemis II landing on its YouTube page beginning at 5:30 p.m. CT, which is embedded below:There are also several platforms to watch Artemis II splashdown coverage, including:Article continues below this ad NASA+ Amazon Prime Apple TV Netflix HBO Max Discovery+ Peacock Roku During the broadcast, speakers will include the crew surgeon, the captain of the USS John P. Murtha, a U.S. Navy diver and others responsible for preparing recovery operations ahead of the crew’s expected return.The crew will undergo medical evaluations in the ship’s infirmary before heading back to shore and flying to NASA’s Johnson Space Center in Houston.Article continues below this adArtemis II landing schedule: See Friday timesAll times Central 12:50 p.m.: Orion cabin configuration for re-entry begins 1:53 p.m.: Return trajectory correction burn 5:30 p.m.: NASA+ coverage begins 6:33 p.m.: Crew module and service module separation 6:37 p.m.: Crew module raise burn 6:53 p.m.: Entry interface 7:07 p.m.: Splashdown in the Pacific Ocean 9:30 p.m.: Post-splashdown news conference at NASA Johnson Space Center Article continues below this adYou can see a live countdown below. Stories you won't want to miss, handpicked by Statesman editors.April 10, 2026Marley Malenfant is a 10-year multimedia journalist who’s worked in print, digital, and broadcast. His prior work includes working in West Texas as a TV reporter for KTXS. During his time there, he covered education, sports, features, and breaking News. He’s skilled in front of the camera as well as behind it. He was a freelance writer, published in D Magazine, Dallas Morning News, SB Nation, Dallas Observer, and was a personality for “The Coach Brothers” sports radio show. Outside of journalism, he was an educator in the Dallas area. He was a 2012 Mayborn Literary Nonfiction Conference grant recipient. In 2021, he was part of the NABJ student multimedia project. He graduated with a paralegal degree from El Centro in 2023. Currently, he’s a trending news reporter for the Austin American-Statesman, covering breaking News, sports, music, and legal trends.",
